smoltcp: a SYN goes to its connection before a listener

A TCP segment goes to the first socket of the set that accepts it, and a
listener accepts any SYN to its port. netd re-arms a fresh listener
beside each connection it takes off one, in whichever slot of the set is
free, often one before the connection's. A SYN the peer sent again (its
first SYN-ACK slow or lost; under QEMU, slirp retransmits on the host's
clock while the guest stands still) then reached that listener, which
opened a second half-open connection for the same addresses and answered
a SYN-ACK of its own; the peer's reset to that one reset the first
connection too, its data unsent (a listen test's write failed with
EPIPE). Segments now go to a socket that is not listening first, then
to the listeners, as Linux looks up a connection before a listener. With
a test (tests/ipv4.rs). Derived from smoltcp, so under smoltcp's license
(0BSD).

